   "id": "e1",
   "source_id": "3.1.1",
   "source_number": "3.1.1",
   "kind": "inline-exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "inline",
    "after": "ex-3-1-6"
   },
   "cite": "ex-3-1-6",
   "prompt": "<p>Plug in and check that this really is the solution.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Differentiate $\\kx = \\frac{2e^{-2t} + e^t}{3}$ and $\\ky = \\frac{-e^{-2t} + e^t}{3}$: $\\kxprime = \\frac{-4e^{-2t} + e^t}{3}$ and $\\kyprime = \\frac{2e^{-2t} + e^t}{3}$. Then $2\\ky - \\kx = \\frac{-2e^{-2t} + 2e^t - 2e^{-2t} - e^t}{3} = \\frac{-4e^{-2t} + e^t}{3} = \\kxprime$, and $\\kyprime = \\frac{2e^{-2t} + e^t}{3} = \\kx$, so both equations hold. At $t = 0$, $\\kx(0) = \\frac{2 + 1}{3} = 1$ and $\\ky(0) = \\frac{-1 + 1}{3} = 0$, so the initial conditions hold too: it really is the solution."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "source_id": "3.1.2",
   "source_number": "3.1.2",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "ex-3-1-1",
   "prompt": "<p>Find the general solution of $\\kxoneprime = \\kxtwo - \\kxone + t$, $\\kxtwoprime = \\kxtwo$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Solve the second equation first, as in Example 3.1.1: $\\kxtwoprime = \\kxtwo$ gives $\\kxtwo = C_2e^t$. The first equation becomes $\\kxoneprime + \\kxone = C_2e^t + t$, a linear first-order equation. With the integrating factor $e^t$, $(e^t\\kxone)' = C_2e^{2t} + te^t$, so $e^t\\kxone = \\frac{C_2}{2}e^{2t} + (t - 1)e^t + C_1$. The general solution is $\\kxone = C_1e^{-t} + \\frac{C_2}{2}e^{t} + t - 1$, $\\kxtwo = C_2e^{t}$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e3",
   "source_id": "3.1.3",
   "source_number": "3.1.3",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "ex-3-1-6",
   "prompt": "<p>Find the general solution of $\\kxoneprime = 3 \\kxone - \\kxtwo + e^t$, $\\kxtwoprime = \\kxone$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Work as in Example 3.1.6. Differentiating the second equation, $\\kxtwodprime = \\kxoneprime = 3\\kxone - \\kxtwo + e^t = 3\\kxtwoprime - \\kxtwo + e^t$, so $\\kxtwodprime - 3\\kxtwoprime + \\kxtwo = e^t$. The characteristic equation $r^2 - 3r + 1 = 0$ has the roots $r = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}$, and a particular solution $Ae^t$ needs $A - 3A + A = 1$, so $A = -1$. Hence $\\kxtwo = C_1e^{\\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}t} + C_2e^{\\frac{3-\\sqrt{5}}{2}t} - e^t$, and from $\\kxone = \\kxtwoprime$, $\\kxone = \\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}C_1e^{\\frac{3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}t} + \\frac{3-\\sqrt{5}}{2}C_2e^{\\frac{3-\\sqrt{5}}{2}t} - e^t$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e4",
   "source_id": "3.1.4",
   "source_number": "3.1.4",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "new-variables",
   "prompt": "<p>Write $a\\kydprime + b\\kyprime + c\\ky = f(x)$ as a first-order system of ODEs.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "The equation is second order (take $a \\neq 0$), so two new variables are needed: $\\kuone = \\ky$ and $\\kutwo = \\kyprime$. Then $\\kuoneprime = \\kutwo$, and solving the equation for $\\kydprime = \\kutwoprime$ gives the system $\\kuoneprime = \\kutwo$, $\\kutwoprime = \\frac{1}{a}\\bigl(f(x) - b\\kutwo - c\\kuone\\bigr)$. Its second equation is the original one, $a\\kydprime + b\\kyprime + c\\ky = f(x)$, written in the $u$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5",
   "source_id": "3.1.5",
   "source_number": "3.1.5",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "higher-order-systems",
   "prompt": "<p>Write $\\kxdprime + \\ky^2 \\kyprime - \\kx^3 = \\sin(t)$, $\\kydprime + {(\\kxprime+\\kyprime)}^2 -\\kx = 0$ as a first-order system of ODEs.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Two second-order equations in two unknowns give four first-order equations: let $\\kuone = \\kx$, $\\kutwo = \\kxprime$, $\\kuthree = \\ky$, $\\kufour = \\kyprime$. Solving each equation for its second derivative, $\\kxdprime = \\kx^3 - \\ky^2\\kyprime + \\sin(t)$ and $\\kydprime = \\kx - (\\kxprime + \\kyprime)^2$, so the system is $\\kuoneprime = \\kutwo$, $\\kutwoprime = \\kuone^3 - \\kuthree^2\\kufour + \\sin(t)$, $\\kuthreeprime = \\kufour$, $\\kufourprime = \\kuone - (\\kutwo + \\kufour)^2$."
   }
  },
  {
